<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raymond of Poitiers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1115–<a href="/years/1149">1149</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> was prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> from 1136 to 1149, a younger son of Duke William IX of Aquitaine who married the heiress Constance. In 1137 he had to accept the overlordship of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a>. He hosted his niece <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> and was killed fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> at the Battle of Inab.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers.jpg" alt="Raymond of Poitiers" width="289" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raymond_of_Poitiers_with_John_II_Komnenos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He defeated the Crusaders at Edessa in 1146, beat <b>Raymond of Poitiers</b>, Prince of Antioch, at the battle of Inab in 1149, took the remnants of the County of Edessa west of the Euphrates in 1150, and in 1154 captured Damascus from the Turkic Burid dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Second Crusade, the Zengid army of Nur al-Din Zengi destroyed the army of <b>Raymond of Poitiers</b>, Prince of Antioch, and the Assassins of Ali ibn-Wafa at Inab.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-29">29 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its army was crushed at the Field of Blood in 1119, and in 1137 <b>Raymond of Poitiers</b> had to accept Byzantine overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Principality of Antioch</a></li></ul></section>
